    "Verified empirically on branch fd20b30 (this card's commit): created a fixture repo with u.md containing '# Café notes / The naïve zqxwvuniq body lives here.', ran `clojure -M:run -- ingest <repo> --profile local --index-dir <tmp>` — output: 'Sections extracted: 0, Extraction failures: 1', EXIT=0. `search zqxwvuniq --mode lexical` and even `search Caf` (the heading) return 0 results. The entire document is absent from the index with a success exit code. Code reading confirms the mechanism: (1) shape/markdown.clj:38-61 converts flexmark char offsets to UTF-8 BYTE offsets (:span/start-byte), so all recorded spans are byte offsets; (2) the NEW code this commit added, lucene.clj:45-55 section-body-text, slices with raw (subs content start end) — char-based — and its guard (<= 0 start end (count content)) compares byte offsets against a char count, so for non-ASCII docs it returns nil (silent fallback to heading+path, i.e. the exact story-03a bug this card's completion comment claims to fix) or slices shifted garbage; (3) grandfathered section-content-hash (section_extraction.clj:130) does the same raw subs and throws StringIndexOutOfBoundsException whenever the first section's body-span end-byte exceeds the char count, which extract-revision (extraction_projection.clj:95-100) catches and demotes to a per-revision failure — so the whole extraction record (and hence all indexing) is lost before section-body-text is even reached. Refutation angles all fail: not factually wrong (reproduced); not documented (card comments mention only the :local duplication caveat, nothing about Unicode/byte-vs-char); not out of scope (the card's core deliverable is 'ep ingest + durable Lucene search works end-to-end', its acceptance criterion is ingest-then-search returning the ingested section, and its own completion comment claims the body-text indexing fix — the new section-body-text is this commit's own defective code, and the card is precisely what makes the grandfathered hash bug production-reachable, per its Intent section: extraction was 'only reachable from tests' before); not minor (any Markdown file with a single non-ASCII character — utterly common prose — silently vanishes from the index with exit 0, while the repo legislates Unicode exactness in AGENTS.md). The correct slicer (shape.markdown/slice, markdown.clj:269-274) exists and is unused at both call sites. Real, in-scope, promotion-gating: blocker stands."]}
  {:title
   "ep ingest at its default profile (:services) always crashes registering any repo — repository-metadata-file/read! slurps a file that never exists",
   :file "src/epiphany/infra/main.clj",
   :detail
   "make-ingest-adapters wires the :services repository-metadata port as {:read repository-metadata-file/read! :write repository-metadata-file/write!} (main.clj:488-490). repository-metadata-file/read! (repository_metadata_file.clj:27-29) unconditionally slurps <common-git-dir>/corpus-archaeology/repository.edn; registration/register! calls :read before :write (registration.clj:22), so on any repo where the file does not yet exist read! throws FileNotFoundException and ingest exits 1 with 'Error: .../repository.edn (No such file or directory)'. Reproduced directly: (reg/register! {:git {...} :repository-metadata {:read rmf/read! :write rmf/write! ...} :observations ...} {:repository-path ...}) => THROWS java.io.FileNotFoundException. Worse, the file can never come into existence via the CLI: ep register --profile services (main.clj:134-136) and ep serve --profile services (main.clj:701-703) stub the metadata port with (fn [_] nil) reads/writes, and ingest's real write! is unreachable because read! throws first. So the default 'ep ingest <path>' invocation is broken for every repository, not just first-time ones. Fix: read! should return nil when the file is absent (analogous to the in-memory port), letting register! fall through to write!.",
   :severity "blocker",
   :card "eng-003g",
   :lens "correctness",
   :survives true,
   :reasons
   ["Claim verified by direct reproduction, not just code reading. (1) `ep ingest` defaults `--profile` to `:services` (main.clj:451-452) and `make-ingest-adapters` wires the real `repository-metadata-file/read!` (main.clj:488-489), which unconditionally slurps `<common-git-dir>/corpus-archaeology/repository.edn` (repository_metadata_file.clj:27-29). (2) `registration/register!` calls `:read` at line 21 and only reaches `:write` at line 34 when read returned nil — so on any repo lacking the file, read! throws first. Reproduced with the exact port functions the CLI wires: `(reg/register! {:repository-metadata {:read rmf/read! :write rmf/write! ...} ...} {:repository-path <fresh-repo>})` => `java.io.FileNotFoundException .../corpus-archaeology/repository.edn (No such file or directory)`; `run-ingest` catches it at main.clj:622 and exits 1 with exactly the error string the reviewer quotes. (3) The file is not created by the normal documented flow: `ep register --profile services` (main.clj:134-136) and `ep serve --profile services` (main.clj:701) stub the metadata port with `(fn [_] nil)`, so register-then-ingest at the default profile always crashes. (The claim's 'never via the CLI' is mildly overstated — `resolve-repository` in infra/repository_identity.clj does handle-missing-and-create, used by seed-candidate/inbox-decide — but ingest does not use it, so the core defect stands.) (4) In-scope and promotion-gating: this card's scope item 1 is literally the `ep ingest` subcommand with idempotent registration, its own completion note directs users to `:services` for incremental ingestion ('Use :services (MongoDB)... :local is one-shot/demo'), yet the only end-to-end test (ingest-local-then-lexical-search-finds-section, main_test.clj:233-237) exercises `-p local`; the default `:services` registration path is untested and broken for every fresh repository. No card comment documents this as an accepted tradeoff. The suggested fix (read! returns nil when absent, matching the in-memory port at in_memory.clj:38-39 and registration's `(when-not existing-metadata ...)` fallthrough) matches the evident design intent."
    "Reproduced the defect directly: (registration/register! ... {:repository-metadata {:read rmf/read! :write rmf/write!} ...}) on a fresh fixture repo throws java.io.FileNotFoundException for <common-git-dir>/corpus-archaeology/repository.edn. Code inspection confirms the chain: run-ingest (main.clj:578) calls register!, which calls (:read repository-metadata) before any :write (registration.clj:21,33-34); repository-metadata-file/read! (lines 27-29) slurps unconditionally with no existence check; ingest's default profile is :services (main.clj:452), which wires the real file adapter (main.clj:488-489). The file can never come into existence via the CLI because ep register --profile services (main.clj:134-136) and the pre-existing serve path stub the metadata port with (fn [_] nil) reads/writes, and ingest's own write! is unreachable since read! throws first. Refutation grounds all fail: (1) the claim is factually correct, verified by execution; (2) the behavior is not documented as a tradeoff — the card's IMPLEMENTED comment documents only a :local duplicate-docs caveat and actively recommends ':services (MongoDB) for incremental ingestion', i.e. the broken path; (3) it is squarely in scope — scope item 1 covers 'registers the repository (idempotent)' with ':services MongoDB' persistence, and git history shows this card's commit fd20b30 is the first ever to wire repository-metadata-file into a reachable production path (pre-fd20b30 main.clj stubbed the port everywhere), so the grandfathered read! became a live bug through this card's own wiring; (4) it is not minor — the headline feature's default invocation (ep ingest <path>) fails on every repository that has never been ingested, which is all of them; the acceptance-criteria unit test only exercised -p local, sidestepping the default profile. The suggested fix (read! returns nil when absent, matching the in-memory port's contract) is correct and small."]}
  {:title
   "Ollama availability probe hardcodes 127.0.0.1:11434, duplicating/bypassing the adapter's configurable :base-url",
   :file "src/epiphany/infra/main.clj",
   :detail
   "ollama-reachable? (added in this commit) opens a raw socket to hardcoded \"127.0.0.1\" 11434, while the embeddings adapter it gates is constructed via `(ollama/make-embeddings-adapter {})` whose :base-url defaults to \"http://localhost:11434\" and is configurable (src/epiphany/infra/adapters/ollama.clj:49-50). There are now two independent sources of truth for the Ollama endpoint; if the adapter is ever pointed elsewhere (config boundary, US-000C direction in AGENTS.md: 'Connection settings come only from the profile/config boundary ... never hardcode'), require-ollama! will report UNAVAILABLE against the wrong socket (or vice versa). The probe should derive host/port from the same configuration as the adapter.",
   :severity "nit",
   :card "eng-003g",
   :lens "architecture-law",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["The factual observation is accurate (ollama-reachable? at main.clj:371-378 opens a socket to hardcoded 127.0.0.1:11434 while the adapter at adapters/ollama.clj:49-50 defaults :base-url to http://localhost:11434), but the claimed defect is hypothetical and self-classified as a nit. Nothing in the delivered code ever points the adapter anywhere else: make-durable-index-ports (main.clj:388) is the only construction site and passes {}, so the adapter always uses the default localhost:11434 — the exact same loopback service the probe checks (127.0.0.1 and localhost resolve to the same local Ollama). There is no config plumbing anywhere in src/ that exposes an Ollama endpoint (grep confirms base-url appears only in the adapter's own defaults), so there are not 'two independent sources of truth' in operation today — there is one effective endpoint, expressed twice consistently. The AGENTS.md config-boundary language the claim cites refers to MongoDB connection settings (US-000C), and no card currently delivers Ollama endpoint configurability; when such a card lands, wiring the probe to the same config is naturally part of it. The behavior also matches the card's stated acceptance criterion ('fail explicitly UNAVAILABLE when Ollama is unreachable'), which is demonstrated. A speculative future-divergence nit over two equivalent loopback spellings is not promotion-gating for review→done."
    "The literal divergence exists (probe at main.clj:376 uses 127.0.0.1:11434; adapter default is http://localhost:11434), but it is neither a real defect nor promotion-gating. (1) No configuration path exists: every call site constructs the adapter as (ollama/make-embeddings-adapter {}) with empty opts, so the two literals can never actually diverge at runtime — the 'two sources of truth' is purely hypothetical until some future card plumbs a :base-url override, and updating the probe belongs to that card's scope, not ENG-003G. (2) The probe follows the repo's established, already-accepted pattern: the US-000C readiness-diagnostics namespace (status: done) hardcodes TCP probes to 127.0.0.1:27017 for MongoDB the same way (services.clj tcp-reachable?/mongo-available?), and mongo/connect! likewise defaults to a literal mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017. A diagnostics-layer loopback default matching the adapter default is the house pattern, not a bypass of the config boundary — the config boundary governs selectable profiles, and no Ollama endpoint config exists anywhere to bypass. (3) The probe is what delivers the card's acceptance criterion 'semantic/hybrid search with Ollama down exits 1 with explicit UNAVAILABLE, never silent fallback,' and the card documents this behavior. (4) The reviewer themselves rated it 'nit' — by the review bar, a latent-consistency nit with zero runtime effect today does not gate a review→done promotion."]}
  {:title
   "--embed path never stamps :embedding-version on embedding records, so every indexed embedding doc gets embedding_version \"null\" and --embedding-version filtering silently matches nothing",
   :file "src/epiphany/infra/main.clj",
   :detail
   "embed-extractions! (main.clj, new in this commit) passes ollama embed-sections! output straight to (:index-embeddings! (:index adapters)). The ollama adapter's embed-sections! returns records with :embedding/vector, :embedding/model, :embedding/dimensions but no :embedding-version (ollama.clj:55-94; the version is only exposed via the separate :embedding-version port fn, ollama.clj:103). lucene embedding->doc writes (str (:embedding-version embedding)) (lucene.clj:125-127) => the literal string \"null\" for every doc. Consequently 'ep search --mode semantic --embedding-version N' (a shipped CLI flag, main.clj:302-304) filters on embedding_version = str N and silently returns zero semantic results against any corpus indexed via 'ep ingest --embed'. Fix: in embed-extractions!, assoc :embedding-version from ((:embedding-version (:embeddings adapters))) onto each record before indexing.",
   :severity "should-fix",
   :card "eng-003g",
   :lens "correctness",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["Verified line-by-line; the claim is factually correct and in-scope. (1) ollama.clj:85-92 — embed-sections! returns records with :embedding/vector, :embedding/model, :embedding/dimensions and no :embedding-version; the version lives only behind the separate :embedding-version port fn (ollama.clj:103-104, a hash of {:model :dimensions}). (2) main.clj:541-542 — embed-extractions!, new in this card's commit fd20b30, pipes those records straight into (:index-embeddings! (:index adapters)) without associng the version. (3) lucene.clj:125-127 — embedding->doc writes (str (:embedding-version embedding)), i.e. the literal string \"null\" for every doc produced via 'ep ingest --embed', the only production embedding-indexing path (which this card delivers as explicit Scope item 1). (4) lucene.clj:247-248 + 177-180 — knn-search adds a TermQuery on embedding_version=str(N) when the request carries :embedding-version, and main.clj:368-369 forwards the shipped --embedding-version CLI flag into the request, so any explicit version filter silently matches zero docs against any corpus embedded through this card's CLI path. Not grandfathered: the mis-stamping caller is this card's own new function, and the card's comment history documents other fixes/limitations but never this tradeoff. It also contradicts the card's own touted principle (explicit, never-silent failure modes) and breaks the version mechanism that exists to detect model changes in a rebuildable-projection architecture; the durable index is left polluted with embedding_version=\"null\". Not minor: a shipped flag on the delivered semantic-search path is silently dead, and the fix is a one-line assoc in this card's code. Survives as a real, in-scope, promotion-gating should-fix."
    "The mechanism described is real (ollama.clj embed-sections! omits :embedding-version; lucene.clj:126 stringifies it to \"null\"), but it does not gate this card's promotion. (1) The default path is unaffected: lucene's knn-search only adds the embedding_version FILTER clause when the request carries :embedding-version (lucene.clj:247-248), and build-search-request only assoc's it when the user explicitly passes --embedding-version — so `ep ingest --embed` followed by `ep search --mode semantic` returns results correctly, which is the behavior this card's scope and acceptance criteria cover. (2) The --embedding-version flag and its filter plumbing are pre-existing context lines in this commit's diff (git show fd20b30: the flag at main.clj:302-304 and build-search-request assoc are unchanged '+'-free context), grandfathered from the earlier hybrid-search card — and the version value is `(hash {:model model :dimensions dimensions})`, an undiscoverable Java hash with no CLI surface to learn it, so the flag was already unusable-in-practice before this card; this commit does not regress any working workflow. (3) The missing key originates in the ollama adapter's record shape, delivered by an earlier story (03b/03c); this card's new embed-extractions! faithfully passes through the adapter's documented output. (4) All four acceptance criteria (lexical ingest→search round-trip, ingest idempotency, explicit UNAVAILABLE when Ollama is down, green unit tests + zero new warnings) are met and evidenced. This is a legitimate should-fix polish item for a follow-up (stamp the version at the port boundary or in the adapter), but it is an opt-in, effectively-undiscoverable flag on an otherwise-working feature — too minor and too grandfathered to block a review→done transition."]}
  {:title
   "ep ingest carries no request/idempotency ID — each re-run records a duplicate repository-location observation",
   :file "src/epiphany/infra/main.clj",
   :detail
   "run-ingest calls `(registration/register! adapters {:repository-path repository-path})` with no :request-id, and ingest-options has no --request-id flag (contrast run-register, which threads `(:request-id options)`). register! dedupes only via `((:find-by-request-id observations) request-id)`; with request-id nil, every ingest run records a fresh `:repository/location-observed` observation (`:observation/request-id (random-uuid)`) into MongoDB. AGENTS.md 'Identity & idempotency': 'Every durable command carries a request/idempotency ID; retry must not duplicate anything.' Revision-at-path and extraction records are deduped by identity keys, but the registration observation is not, so retries accumulate duplicate durable observations for the same repository.",
   :severity "should-fix",
   :card "eng-003g",
   :lens "architecture-law",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["Claim verified against the code. src/epiphany/infra/main.clj:578 calls `(registration/register! adapters {:repository-path repository-path})` with no :request-id, and ingest-options has no --request-id flag (unlike run-register at lines 89-109, which threads `(:request-id options)`). application/registration.clj dedupes only via `((:find-by-request-id observations) request-id)`; with request-id nil the Mongo adapter's find-by-request-id (mongo.clj:608-615) filters on `(str nil)` and never matches, so register! falls through to record a fresh `:repository/location-observed` observation with `:observation/request-id (random-uuid)` every run. The only unique index on that collection is on request_id (mongo.clj:142-146), so no dup-key protection applies — each `ep ingest` re-run under :services appends a duplicate durable observation for the same repository/path. Not grandfathered: run-ingest is new code delivered by this card. Not documented as an accepted tradeoff: the card's completion comment documents only the :local in-memory caveat, not this. In scope: the card's Scope bullet 1 explicitly promises 'registers the repository (idempotent)', and AGENTS.md's Identity & idempotency rule states 'Every durable command carries a request/idempotency ID; retry must not duplicate anything' — ep ingest is a durable command (MongoDB + durable Lucene) that violates this on retry. Acceptance criterion 2 only covers revision-at-path/extraction, but the scope bullet and the repo-wide invariant make this a genuine, promotion-relevant gap; the fix (thread an optional --request-id into the register! call, mirroring run-register) is small."
    "The claim is factually accurate about the mechanism but does not gate promotion. (1) Out of scope as delimited by the card itself: the acceptance criteria explicitly scope ingest idempotency to 'revision-at-path observations or extraction records (idempotency by observation identity keys)' — both verified deduped by identity keys in the implementation. The scope line 'registers the repository (idempotent)' refers to idempotent resource-id resolution, which register! does achieve: re-runs resolve the same resource-id via the common-git-dir repository.edn metadata (ADR-001), so no functional state diverges. (2) Grandfathered pattern: the nil-request-id → random-uuid behavior is ENG-001A's deliberate port design (idempotency is opt-in via caller-supplied request-id; in_memory.clj lines 70-86 documents 'No request-id — validate and store unconditionally'), and the HTTP register endpoint (infra/http.clj:258, committed in ba2d7da, an ancestor of this card's fd20b30) already calls register! the same way without a request-id. Holding ENG-003G responsible for a pre-existing cross-boundary design choice misattributes the finding. (3) Impact is log noise, not corruption: each re-ingest under :services appends one append-only :repository/location-observed document carrying the same resource-id; nothing resolves, ranks, or dedupes incorrectly as a result, and the :local-profile duplication caveat is already documented in the card's completion note. If a --request-id flag or resource-id-keyed location dedup is desired, that is an ENG-001A-contract-level enhancement card, not a defect in this card's delivered scope."]}
  {:title
   "AC3's UNAVAILABLE failure path is only conditionally tested — never exercised when Ollama is up",
   :file "test/epiphany/infra/main_test.clj",
   :detail
   "search-semantic-requires-ollama-or-fails-explicitly branches on the actual outcome: `(if (zero? exit) (is ... \"results\") (do (is (= 1 exit)) (is ... \"Ollama\")))`. AGENTS.md states Ollama is running locally on this machine, so the green 691-test run almost certainly took the success branch and never asserted exit 1 / explicit UNAVAILABLE. The code path (require-ollama! throwing {:code :unavailable} before hs/search) is correct on inspection, but the repo's own standard ('tested, not asserted') would want a deterministic test, e.g. with-redefs on #'main/ollama-reachable? returning false.",
   :severity "nit",
   :card "eng-003g",
   :lens "dod-coverage",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["The finding is factually accurate about the test's conditional structure, but it fails the promotion-gating bar on every other axis. (1) The reviewer concedes the production code path is correct on inspection: require-ollama! throws {:code :unavailable} before any search call, which is exactly what AC3 demands ('exits 1 with an explicit UNAVAILABLE-style error, not a fallback to lexical'). (2) The failure-path assertions do exist in the test — `(is (= 1 exit))` and `(is (string/includes? out \"Ollama\"))` — and they execute whenever Ollama is unreachable; the test is environment-conditional, not absent. AGENTS.md itself frames Ollama as an ambient local service, so an environment-dependent test of an integration with an ambient service is a defensible phase-1 choice; the card's own hygiene note shows the author deliberately made tests hermetic where it mattered (--index-dir, --mode lexical) and left the Ollama-dependent path conditional. (3) The reviewer self-classifies the severity as 'nit'. A nit that acknowledges correct behavior and proposes a nice-to-have improvement (with-redefs on ollama-reachable?) is precisely the category the skeptic instructions say must not gate a review→done promotion — it is a follow-up chore candidate, not a defect against this card's acceptance criteria, all of which (lexical ingest→search round trip, idempotency, explicit UNAVAILABLE path present, 691 tests green, zero new warnings) are met per the card's implementation record."
    "The claim's factual core is accurate — search-semantic-requires-ollama-or-fails-explicitly (main_test.clj:168-179) does branch on the actual exit code, and on this machine (Ollama up per AGENTS.md) the failure-path assertions likely never execute. But the finding fails the promotion-gating bar. (1) The production behavior AC3 demands is verifiably present in the code the card delivered: run-search calls require-ollama! (main.clj:424-425) before constructing ports or invoking hs/search, and require-ollama! (main.clj:390-397) throws ex-info with {:code :unavailable} and an explicit message naming Ollama — exactly the 'exits 1 with an explicit UNAVAILABLE-style error, not a fallback to lexical' criterion, which the reviewer themselves concedes is 'correct on inspection.' (2) The failure-path assertions exist in the test and execute whenever Ollama is unreachable; the test is environment-conditional, not missing. AGENTS.md frames Ollama as an ambient local service, so an environment-dependent test of an ambient-service integration is a defensible phase-1 choice, and the card's implementation record shows deliberate hermetic-ization where it mattered (temp --index-dir, --mode lexical). (3) The reviewer self-classifies this as a 'nit' proposing a nice-to-have (with-redefs on ollama-reachable?). A nit that acknowledges correct behavior and suggests a determinism improvement is a follow-up chore candidate, not a defect against any acceptance criterion — all of which (lexical ingest→search round trip, idempotency, explicit UNAVAILABLE path present, 691 tests green, zero new warnings) are demonstrated in the card's implementation record. Not promotion-gating for review→done."]}
  {:title
   "ep ingest with default :services profile crashes on first run against a fresh repo — registration can never create repository.edn",
   :file "src/epiphany/infra/main.clj",
   :detail
   "make-ingest-adapters wires the REAL repository-metadata-file port for :services (`:read repository-metadata-file/read!`), but read! (src/epiphany/infra/repository_metadata_file.clj:27-29) slurps `<common-git-dir>/corpus-archaeology/repository.edn` unconditionally and throws java.io.FileNotFoundException when the file does not exist yet. registration/register! calls (:read repository-metadata) BEFORE the `(when-not existing-metadata ((:write ...)))` that would create the file, so first-ever registration via ingest is unreachable. Verified directly: `(reg/register! {:repository-metadata {:read mf/read! :write mf/write! ...}} ...)` on a fresh dir throws `java.io.FileNotFoundException \"/tmp/fresh-repo-git/corpus-archaeology/repository.edn (No such file or directory)\"`. Since ingest-options defaults `--profile` to :services (main.clj:453), the headline command `ep ingest <path>` fails in its default configuration on any never-registered repository; the raw IO exception also escapes the UNAVAILABLE/typed-error contract (caught only by the generic Exception handler). All ingest tests use `-p local` (test/epiphany/infra/main_test.clj:237), whose in-memory metadata port returns nil on first read, so the gap is untested. This commit is the first to wire repository-metadata-file into a CLI path (run-register :services still uses nil-returning stubs), so the violation is new. Violates the card's own scope item 1 ('registers the repository (idempotent)') and ADR-001's identity flow (first contact must mint + persist the resource-id). Fix: read! should return nil when the file is absent (or register! should tolerate the missing file).",
   :severity "blocker",
   :card "eng-003g",
   :lens "architecture-law",
   :survives true,
